Original Parla assets
---------------------
Everything below was made for Parla and is licensed like the code
(MPL-2.0), unless a different licence is named. No Slack artwork is used.

- assets/icons/ui: original 24 px line icons, drawn for Parla.
- assets/icons/app, assets/icons/tray, assets/brand: the Parla logo and the
  app and tray icons generated from it.
- assets/themes: the built-in Parla Light, Parla Dark and Parla High Contrast
  themes.
- assets/keymaps: the default macOS, Linux and Windows keymaps.
- assets/settings: the default and initial user settings templates.
- site/public/favicon.svg: the Parla logo, as served by the website.
- site/public/shots and crates/ui_testing/baselines: screenshots of Parla
  rendered from the FakeProvider fixtures; they show only synthetic data.
- crates/domain/src/test_support/solved.png and party.gif: generated 32 px
  test images for the fixture's custom emoji.
- crates/providers/*/tests/fixtures: synthetic and redacted API samples for
  the tests; they contain no third-party creative work.

assets/sounds/parla-chime.wav is original work synthesised by
scripts/gen_chime.ts and released under CC0-1.0; see
assets/sounds/README.md.

assets/fonts bundles Inter 4.1 (Regular, Italic, Medium, SemiBold and Bold)
under the SIL Open Font License 1.1, with the licence text next to the files.
Inter is the default UI font and Parla registers it at start-up; the visual
test harness renders from the same bytes. crates/ui_testing/fonts bundles the
test-only JetBrains Mono and Noto Emoji fonts under the same licence, with the
licence text next to each family. It is never packaged with the app.

Third-party software
--------------------
Parla is built on GPUI through the crates.io snapshots gpui-fast and
gpui-fast-platform (GPUI Fast), and gpui-base (GPUI Base, from the gpui-kit
project), all Apache-2.0, at the exact versions pinned in Cargo.toml. They
depend on the gpui-pre-* utility crates (util, collections, sum-tree and
others), also Apache-2.0 Zed-derived snapshots. No other Zed crate is a
dependency and no code is copied from Zed. Zed's theme and keymap file
formats are implemented from their documented shape.

The app statically links these native libraries, each under a permissive
licence:

- AWS-LC (aws-lc-rs and aws-lc-sys): ISC and Apache-2.0, with BoringSSL and
  OpenSSL heritage. Copyright Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates, Google
  Inc. and the OpenSSL Project.
- OpenSSL 3.6.3 (openssl-src): Apache-2.0.
- SQLCipher 4.20.0 with SQLite 3.54.1 and libtomcrypt 1.18.2
  (sqlcipher-src): SQLCipher is BSD-3-Clause (Copyright (c) 2025 ZETETIC
  LLC), SQLite is in the public domain, and libtomcrypt is WTFPL and
  "blessing".
- libopus 1.6.1 (opusic-sys): BSD-3-Clause, including its royalty-free
  patent licences.

MPL-2.0 crates in the dependency tree (cssparser, cssparser-macros,
selectors, dtoa-short, dwrote and option-ext, plus cbindgen at build time)
are used unmodified;
their source is published on crates.io.

Unicode data and tables in ICU4X, unicode-ident, unicode-properties and
emojis are used under the Unicode License v3 (Copyright (c) Unicode, Inc.).

The Mozilla CA root store shipped by webpki-root-certs is used under the
Community Data License Agreement - Permissive 2.0
(CDLA-Permissive-2.0).

Nothing in the dependency tree is GPL-only, AGPL or SSPL: self_cell offers
Apache-2.0 and r-efi offers MIT/Apache-2.0 alternatives, and Parla uses
those.

crates/chime_signal vendors the Apache-2.0 Chime signalling protobuf from
aws/amazon-chime-sdk-js; see crates/chime_signal/NOTICE. The text system in
ui_testing is ported from gpui-ce under Apache-2.0; see
crates/ui_testing/NOTICE.

The website bundles Inter and JetBrains Mono webfonts from @fontsource
(SIL OFL 1.1) and Lucide icons (ISC). Its build tools are not shipped:
sharp uses libvips (LGPL-3.0-or-later) only to rasterise images at build
time, and the Lighthouse dev dependency reaches Sentry's
FSL-1.1-Apache-2.0 CLI, which the site build never runs.

The full list of third-party crates and their licences is generated with
`cargo about` (about.toml and about.hbs). `mise run release:notice` writes
this file followed by that list to target/release-assets/NOTICE, which is
bundled with the app; `cargo deny list` shows the licences in the tree.
